From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Abhi Teja Cholleti <achollet@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] pwhm: fix WiFi.Reset() EndPoint re-created at wrong indices
 (4,5,6 instead of 1,2,3)

After WiFi.Reset(), EndPoint instances are deleted and the defaults ODL
re-creates them via "instance add (alias)" with no explicit index. amxd
auto-assigns the next index as (template->last_index + 1). Since
last_index is a high-water mark that is never reset on instance deletion,
after the first boot where EndPoints 1,2,3 were created, last_index stays
at 3. The next auto-assign gives 4, 5, 6.

Fix: after s_deleteAllInstances(EndPoint), reset epTmpl->last_index = 0.
With last_index = 0, the next auto-assign starts from 1, restoring the
correct WiFi.EndPoint.1/2/3 indices after reset.

Signed-off-by: Abhi Teja Cholleti <achollet@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
diff --git a/src/wld.c b/src/wld.c
--- a/src/wld.c
+++ b/src/wld.c
@@ -1208,7 +1208,15 @@
             pEP->index = 0;
         }
     }
-    s_deleteAllInstances(amxd_object_findf(get_wld_object(), "EndPoint"));
+    amxd_object_t* epTmpl = amxd_object_findf(get_wld_object(), "EndPoint");
+    s_deleteAllInstances(epTmpl);
+    /* Reset the template's last_index to 0. amxd uses last_index as a
+     * high-water mark and never decrements it on deletion. Auto-assigned
+     * indices (index arg == 0) are computed as last_index+1, so without
+     * this reset the re-created EndPoints land at 4,5,6 instead of 1,2,3. */
+    if(epTmpl != NULL) {
+        epTmpl->last_index = 0;
+    }

     /*
      * Walk the entire WiFi object tree and remove all registered action
